{
  "question": "Does a periodically probed, steadily converting material patch deliver wave work, and what supplies the energy?",
  "working_base": "1dc45d09de9a8ed1d02337cba71a123d090453f7",
  "preparation": "Continuously fed planar band, order .85 to .45, constant density one, intake .25, radius-one cross-section, fixed endpoint order; the relative slip is spatially uniform. Angular frequency from the archived radius-one real scalar; using its value does not determine the local/global mode overlap. Local amplitude is in coupon units.",
  "hypotheses": "Direct shear energy chi*s^2/2, or contact energy alpha*chi*(s-p)^2/2 with D_t p=(s-p)/tau - beta*(s-p)*D_t[1-H(chi)], zero extension at entry. h*H(chi) per particle is an additional supplied free-energy store. Native pure-phase order energy difference is zero. h=1.01 h_ref and h=0 controls; both hold endpoint order. No spontaneous source or independent microscopic calibration of beta or h.",
  "method": "Nonlinear coupled time-periodic front and transported-relaxed-contact equations solved by Fourier phase collocation and axial boundary-value integration. Period means include work delivered to imposed probe, derivative of shear stiffness during conversion, work of a changing relaxed reference, heat, carried elastic energy and signed endpoint work. The weak shear result uses displacement from the unforced front to resolve order-amplitude-four work. Checked weak sign separately by spatial finite difference plus a retarded response, and kernel quadrature on the analytic reference front.",
  "sign": "Positive wave work means material energy delivered to the oscillatory probe. Outward order work and incremental carried elastic energy point out when positive. Head power equals order heat + outward order work + contact renewal + carried elastic energy increase + wave work. Rates quoted for a sample over the .85 to .45 band. Energy per entering constituent is head times H(.85)-H(.45).",
  "limits": "Periodic driven solutions; dynamical stability, spontaneous formation, nonlinear saturation and the coupling to the actual cavity mode are unverified. No physical 0PN acceleration follows.",
  "guard_sha256": "86be9b55a56f7a3538cdf3852b74562bdc8c5bab19c9ad894cbc2c3b6bbe257b",
